"Internal Error ()\nciteturn202view0 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2907136\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: URL https://doi.org/10.1109/ACCESS.2019.2907136 is not accessible via this tool.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn202view1 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1051200417301380\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1\nL0: Failed to fetch https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S1051200417301380: (403) Forbidden\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHypothesis testing when a nuisance parameter is present only under the alternative - linear model case (https://robertnz.net/pdf/nuisance3.pdf)\nciteturn202view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 8; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://robertnz.net/pdf/nuisance3.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 453\nL0@P0: Hypothesis testing when a nuisance parameter is present only under\nL1@P0: the alternative - linear model case\nL2@P0: By ROBERT B. DAVIES\nL3@P0: Statistics Research Associates Limited\nL4@P0: PO Box 12 649, Thorndon, Wellington, New Zealand\nL5@P0: robert@statsresearch.co.nz\nL6@P0: Summary\nL7@P0: The results of Davies (1977, 1987) are extended to a linear model situation with\nL8@P0: unknown residual variance.\nL9@P0: Some key words: Change point; F-process; Frequency component; Hypothesis\nL10@P0: test; Nuisance parameter; t-process; Two-phase regression; Up-crossing.\nL11@P0: 1. Introduction\nL12@P0: Davies (1977, 1987) introduced a method for testing a hypothesis in the presence\nL13@P0: of a nuisance parameter, θ, which enters into the model only under the alternative.\nL14@P0: Examples 1 and 3 in the second paper were concerned with detecting a discrete fre\u0002quency component and detecting a change in slope. Both are of the form in which we\nL15@P0: observe Y1,...,Yn, denoted by a column vector, Y , which are independently normally\nL16@P0: distributed with variance σ2 and mean\nL17@P0: E(Y )= Xγ + W(θ)ξ, (1)\nL18@P0: where X is an n×s matrix and W(θ) is an n×p matrix function of the scalar nuisance\nL19@P0: parameter θ ∈ [L, U]. The column vectors γ and ξ are unknown parameters. The\nL20@P0: objective is to test the hypothesis ξ =0 against the alternative that at least one\nL21@P0: element of ξ is nonzero. If p =1 we may be interested in testing ξ =0 against the\nL22@P0: one-sided alternative ξ > 0. If ξ =0 then θ does not enter into the model, so the\nL23@P0: model is of the general form considered by Davies (1977, 1987).\nL24@P0: This paper extends the results of Davies (1977, 1987) to the situation when the\nL25@P0: residual variance σ2 is unknown and n is too small for it to be estimated accurately.\nL26@P0: This requires us, in particular, to replace the Gaussian and χ2 processes by t and F\nL27@P0: processes.\nL28@P0: The main results are given in §2, the application to the examples in §3, and the\nL29@P0: derivation of the results in the Appendix. The notation is similar to that in Davies\nL30@P0: (1987). In particular, Y 0 denotes the transpose of a vector Y and kY k its L2 norm.\nL31@P0-1: 12. Main Results\nL32@P1: Assume that q = n − s − p> 0 and that the matrix (X,W(θ)) is of full rank. For\nL33@P1: each θ carry out a QR factorisation of (X,W(θ)) (Golub & Van Loan, 1996, p. 223)\nL34@P1: or some other orthogonalisation procedure so that\nL35@P1: \nL36@P1: \nL37@P1: S\nL38@P1: P(θ)\nL39@P1: Q(θ)\nL40@P1: \nL41@P1:  ¡ XW(θ) ¢\nL42@P1: =\nL43@P1: \nL44@P1: \nL45@P1: GH(θ)\nL46@P1: 0 J(θ)\nL47@P1: 00\nL48@P1: \nL49@P1:  , (2)\nL50@P1: where (S0\nL51@P1: ,P(θ)0,Q(θ)0)0 is an orthogonal matrix and G, H(θ) and J(θ) are s×s, s×p\nL52@P1: and p×p matrices. The s×n matrix S is independent of θ whereas the p×n and q×n\nL53@P1: matrices P(θ) and Q(θ) will usually depend on θ. The QR factorisation (2) provides\nL54@P1: a convenient way of calculating the orthogonal components, SY and Z(θ)= P(θ)Y ,\nL55@P1: of the data vector Y corresponding to X and W(θ) and the component corresponding\nL56@P1: to the residuals R(θ)= Q(θ)Y . Then, for each θ,\nL57@P1: F(θ)= kZ(θ)k2 /p\nL58@P1: kR(θ)k2 /q (3)\nL59@P1: will have an F distribution with p and q degrees of freedom when ξ =0. If θ were\nL60@P1: known then the usual test of the hypothesis ξ =0 would reject the hypothesis when\nL61@P1: (3) was large. Suppose the elements of P(θ) and Q(θ) are continuous functions of θ ∈\nL62@P1: [L, U] and have continuous derivatives except possibly for a finite number of jumps.\nL63@P1: Then we will say that (3) is an F process with p and q degrees of freedom. If p =1\nL64@P1: then we will say\nL65@P1: t(θ)= Z(θ)\nL66@P1: kR(θ)k /q 1\nL67@P1: 2\nL68@P1: (4)\nL69@P1: is a t process with q degrees of freedom. Here, Z(θ) is regarded as a scalar. If θ were\nL70@P1: known then the usual test of the hypothesis ξ =0 against the one-sided alternative\nL71@P1: ξ > 0 would reject the hypothesis when (4) was large. This is assuming that we have\nL72@P1: chosen the signs of the elements of P(θ) so that large values of Z(θ) correspond to\nL73@P1: large values of ξ.\nL74@P1: When θ was not known, the approach of Davies (1977, 1987) would be to use the\nL75@P1: maximum with respect to θ of either (3) or (4) as the test statistic. The remainder of\nL76@P1: this section is concerned with calculating approximations to the distributions of these\nL77@P1: maxima. As in Davies (1977, 1987), these approximations are based on the expected\nL78@P1: number of upcrossings by (3) or (4), regarded as a stochastic process indexed by θ,\nL79@P1: of the critical value.\nL80@P1: Define an analogue of the Beta distribution,\nL81@P1: β(θ)= kZ(θ)k2 /D2, (5)\nL82@P1: where D2 = kZ(θ)k2 + kR(θ)k2 = kY k2 − kSY k2\nL83@P1: . Hence F(θ)= qβ(θ)/[p{1 − β(θ)}].\nL84@P1: Let A(θ)=