le size, n, and\nL237@P3-4: 4nominal significance level. The number of simulations was 100,000. The approximate\nL238@P4: test uses formula (12) and the accurate test uses formula (8). Both of these tests\nL239@P4: show a satisfactory agreement with the nominal significance level. The chi-squared\nL240@P4: versions use formula (3·2) of Davies (1987). The A version divides each S(θ) value\nL241@P4: by the variance of the raw data and the B version by the residual mean square after\nL242@P4: the frequency component corresponding to θ has been fitted. Neither is satisfactory,\nL243@P4: particularly for small values of the significance level even when n = 200.\nL244@P4: Table 1. Actual significance levels for discrete frequency test\nL245@P4: n nominal significance level 20 5 1 0.2\nL246@P4: 10 approximate test 19.5 4.4 0.80 0.13\nL247@P4: accurate test 19.7 4.8 0.95 0.17\nL248@P4: chi-sq test - A 3.2 0.0 0.00 0.00\nL249@P4: chi-sq test - B 66.5 40.3 24.56 15.87\nL250@P4: 50 approximate test 19.4 5.0 0.93 0.18\nL251@P4: accurate test 19.2 4.9 0.92 0.18\nL252@P4: chi-sq test - A 13.0 2.2 0.21 0.01\nL253@P4: chi-sq test - B 33.5 12.3 3.82 1.21\nL254@P4: 200 approximate test 18.5 4.8 0.96 0.17\nL255@P4: accurate test 18.3 4.7 0.94 0.17\nL256@P4: chi-sq test - A 15.6 3.6 0.60 0.09\nL257@P4: chi-sq test - B 23.1 6.8 1.58 0.34\nL258@P4: Appendix\nL259@P4: Derivation of main results\nL260@P4: Assume that P(θ) and Q(θ) defined in §2 are continuous with continuous deriv\u0002atives except possibly for a finite number of jumps in the derivative at fixed values\nL261@P4: of θ. For simplicity assume that σ =1 and drop explicit references to θ. Let η =\nL262@P4: ∂Z/∂θ − A0\nL263@P4: Z and T = ∂β/∂θ =2Z0(∂Z/∂θ)/D2 =2Z0η/D2 since A = P(∂P/∂θ)0 is\nL264@P4: skew-symmetric.\nL265@P4: Theorem A1. The expected number of up-crossings of the level u by the process β(θ)\nL266@P4: defined in formula (5) over the range [L, U] is\nL267@P4: Z U\nL268@P4: L\nL269@P4: ψ(u, θ)dθ, (A1)\nL270@P4: whe